00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.730 align:start position:0%
 
Chancellor in your head of the National  Security Council here clearly there are  a lot of preparations being made for the  battle for mosul so-called are the  kurdish peshmerga going to participate  in that definitely we have looked at the  plan the plan has been prepared and  we've reviewed the plan and definitely  person areas are going to participate  but of course there will be some  limitations of how far they can go  what are those limitations there are  some geographical limitations that we  don't want to to go to the areas that  will probably create some sensitivities  so but basically on the plan it's very  clear how far the peshmerga's can go  there are sensitivities in Mosul because  of the cultural and ethnic sectarian  makeup of that city through one of those  sensitivities is that the Shia militias  from Iraq may be part of this battle  plan what assurances do you have that  that won't be the case see you're right  I mean this sensitivities I have to be  taken into consideration the components  of Mosul is very mixed you have very  different people of different  backgrounds Aetna  ethnic groups religious groups so you  have to be considerate of how  comfortable these people are going to be  with the incoming liberating force some  of the forces that are not well accepted  by the local communities must not try to  get into the city basically to not  irritate the population or to not push  them to collaborate with Isis do you  believe I have no reason not to believe  has the Peshmerga been given the  resources it needs to be an effective  partner  we have always asked for more effective  weapons and resources to the peshmerga's  unfortunately they haven't been given  what they have asked for or what they  have deserve  especially in terms of giving protecting  gears to the peshmerga's or the kind of  equipments that they needed to quickly  defeat Isis kind of weapons that they  needed unfortunately there weren't there  for the peshmergas but peshmerga's were  very determined to fight and defeat  defeat Isis of course they did but it  was a you know a heavy cost and of  course we had many casualties of course  with more adequate better equipments our  casualties would have been much less and  I think the enemy would have been  defeated much quicker so do you feel  used as Kurds no we don't feel used but  we feel as part of this coalition  against terrorism that the international  community should have had more or felt  more responsible to providing what the  peshmerga's needed as the peshmerga's  are fighting not only for their own  cause but on behalf of the rest of the  world why do you think they've been so  reluctant to help there are some you  know like I said political sensitivities  is perhaps but I think these are they  shouldn't have been used as an excuse  because this is a war against terror and  terror is a problem that the whole world  is facing not helping the peshmerga's to  the extent that they needed to be  supported for whatever excuse or reasons  they might come up with I think is not  it's not fair or not logical to know to  be correct however we do understand that  perhaps there are some regional players  that may not be very happy and satisfied  with the kind of weapons that  peshmerga's receive as they think that  the strength of pesh MERGA is not in  their best interest which again I would  definitely argue that that's not the  correct calculation of how things are  because the strength of pesh MERGA is  here the strength of kurdistan  definitely is going to help not only  Iraq but the entire region  when you say regional players do you  mean  predominantly turkey and there's lesser  extent Iran I don't want to name any  country but basically the original  players are very obvious you know in all  the countries that are here and what  preparations are you making for what  some people have wound make could be in  a humanitarian catastrophe we've talked  to the UN we've talked to the coalition  forces we have talked to back that of  course and here in the KRG we've done  all we can so far you know you you know  that we are we are hosting about two  million IDPs and refugees from the rest  of Iraq and also from Syria we expect a  big inflow of people from also coming in  toward here to Kurdistan so there are  camps have been set up but of course we  can provide them shelter we can provide  them security but KRG doesn't have the  capability to take care of them and  provide all the needs for this large  number of people that are coming in you  talk about a short campaign that would  be everybody's preference in Mosul but  the pattern in Fallujah Ramadi is that  it could take years is that something  that you think is conceivable with the  city this big I think it's going to be a  difficult war in Mosul based on the  information that we have they are  preparing to fight very fiercely inside  the city they are digging tunnels they  are preparing themselves to find but at  the end during the war I know you never  know what happens so they could collapse  you don't know and that's what we hope  but we have to be prepared for the worst  and for a long battle doesn't that risk  besieging Mosul and hurting the civilian  population over a longer period well you  know masala has been besieged anyway  based no no besides what Isis has been  trying to provide and do for the people  or let people do for themselves that  that's a better phrase they haven't  allowed any any assistance or any wanted  to go and reach the civil population so  in any case and I mean it's a hard time  to begin with if we talk about where the  Peshmerga the KRG forces are now  they are beyond what used to be your  frontier if you like with Iraq will you  give that territory back there's been  retaken it like I said let's let's be  clear on this we don't think that we  have taken anyone's land we have just  reclaimed what this initially been ours  now we have the chance to make it right  we have made it right and we are going  to defend it so you're not gonna move  absolutely not in that context whether  you're looking at Mosul an amber  province at the relationship with  Kurdistan isn't  Iraq going to face more civil wars even  after the defeat of Isis well let's  let's be honest  Isis is just a by-product it's just a  result of some much bigger problems the  root cause of this problem is political  to begin with if Iraq had worked for the  last hundred years we wouldn't have had  all these problems it didn't work it has  never been peaceful or safe not you know  for their own people or the neighbors so  if you know the mistakes have been made  in the past and now it's time for the  world to realize that there is the time  to look for new approaches for new  solutions if things are not approached  that way then expect problems to  continue expect terrorist organizations  like Isis to emerge again remember I  mean before Isis there was al Qaeda  so after Isis there could be something  else that has the root causes have to be  addressed and and the real problems have  to be solved in order to prevent the the  rise of this sort of extremist  radicalized organizations imagine if if  political structure worked there could  have been economic prosperity they could  have been social order they could have  been peace among all the people so there  would be there would be security  everywhere so there would be no vacuum  for these radical as terrorist  organizations to come and grow

